\begin_layout Section
Finding Demographic Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we describe pseudocode for calculating the demographically stable probabili
ty distribution of population densities across patches and of the transition
 probabilities between patch density states.
 Patches have a population size state 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{0,1,...,I\}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is the fineness of the grid of possible population sizes.
 The actual population size for any given state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{i}=n_{max}\cdot\frac{i}{I}\label{eq:}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{max}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum population size of any patch.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reproduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adults, individuals of size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S$
\end_inset

, reproduce asexually at a rate dependent on their reproductive quality
 state.
 We here define 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m)$
\end_inset

, the expected reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R(s,m)=\begin{cases}
\text{if \ensuremath{s\geq S}}: & r_{b}+m\cdot r_{\text{fighter}}\\
\text{else}: & 0
\end{cases}\label{eq:-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where S is their size at maturity, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline reproductive rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum benefit of reproductive quality boosting phenotypes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dispersal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
State distribution of newborns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally, individuals would be born with precise and accurate prior information
 regarding the propbability distribution over the population density states
 of patches.
 Individuals would then update their information state given cues for the
 population density of their patch which come from the availability of food,
 see section 1.2.
 Individuals are born at size state 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 and reproductive quality state 0.
 However, to form a discrete grid of states that we can solve a Markov decision
 process for we need interpolate each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto a grid of possible discrete probability values (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{0.0,0.1,0.2,...,1.0\}$
\end_inset

).
 Assuming want a fine grid of possible patch densities and of subjective
 probability states, this would generate an intractably large state space
 for us to solve for, and so instead we assume individuals have a more course
 gid of subjective probability states, binning multiple patch density states
 into a fewer state dimensions, with the simplest case being that individuals
 perceive the population density simply as being either low or high and
 so will inherit a binomial prior belief.
 The value that needs to be discretised will therefore be defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta=\sum_{i}^{I/2}h(q_{i})\label{eq:-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

such that we sum probabilities over all population densities up to which
 will interpolate on the grid of belief states 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{0/L,1/L,...,L/L\}$
\end_inset

 by using the following algorithm
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{1}=\text{floor}(\eta\cdot L)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{2}=d_{1}+1
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{2}=\eta\cdot L-d_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}=1-p_{2}\label{eq:-19}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so for all states 
