, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)=0$
\end_inset

, and then update given these interpolated priors
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\rho}(s=1,\pi=d_{1},m=0)+=p_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\rho}(s=1,\pi=d_{2},m=0)+=p_{2}\label{eq:-20}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Patch Projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming a dispersal rate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

, the number of individuals arriving in each patch of population density
 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\alpha(q_{i})=(1-\mu)\cdot q_{i}\cdot r(q_{i})+\mu\cdot h(q_{i})\cdot\sum_{j=0}^{I}h(q_{j})\cdot q_{j}\cdot r(q_{j})\label{eq:-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r(q)$
\end_inset

 is the expected reproductive rate of an individual in a patch of popuation
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $h(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The expected reproductive rate of an individual in a patch of popuation
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r(q_{i})=\sum_{s}^{S}\sum_{\pi}^{\Pi}\sum_{m}^{M}[\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)\cdot R(s,m,i)]\label{eq:-5}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability that an individual will be of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given that they are in a patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m,i)$
\end_inset

 is the reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 (see section 1.4).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following arrivals, the new population density of patches of prior density
 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 become 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{i}^{\prime}=min(q_{I},q_{i}+\alpha(q_{i}))\label{eq:-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We must then interpolate the posterior population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 back on to the grid of density states, with the nearest positions being
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
d_{1}(i)=floor(\frac{q_{i}^{\prime}}{n_{max}}\cdot I)\label{eq:-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
d_{2}(i)=d_{1}(i)+1.\label{eq:-8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore patches of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 transition to patches of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{2}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{2}(i)=(\frac{q_{i}^{\prime}}{n_{max}}\cdot I)-d_{1}(i),\label{eq:-9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and transition to density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{1}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}(i)=1-p_{2}(i).\label{eq:-10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then returns the transition probabilities between patch density states
 given dispersal 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(i)}|q_{i})$
\end_inset

, which at demographic stability will be used for finding the best response
 strategy.
 For all 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(i)}|q_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}(i)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(i)}|q_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}(i)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{2}(i)}|q_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior probability distribution over patch population density states
 is then defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h^{\prime}(q_{i})=\sum_{j}^{I}h(q_{j})\cdot p_{births}(q_{i}|q_{j})\label{eq:-11}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Population Projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So that we can track the growth rate of patches we must know the probability
 distribution of individual states within patches of each given size, so
 for patches of size 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 (to remind you) we define distribution over all possible state combinations
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)\text{: the proportion of individuals in patches of size \ensuremath{q_{i}} that are of state \ensuremath{\{s,\pi,m\}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals in any patch state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 will, given arrivals from reproduction, transition to patch state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{1}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}(i)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{2}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}(i)$
\end_inset

.
 Arrivals will also be added, with the posterior distribution as weighted
 by the change in population size thusly
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho_{d_{1}(i)}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)=\frac{i}{d_{1}(i)}\cdot\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{d_{1}(i)-i}{d_{1}(i)}\cdot\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)\label{eq:-12}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho_{d_{2}(i)}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)=\frac{i}{d_{2}(i)}\cdot\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{d_{2}(i)-i}{d_{2}(i)}\cdot\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)\label{eq:-13}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

for a newborn (as all arrivals are).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foraging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals observe cues for population density in the form of food availability.
 Food availability is a random variable influenced by the population density,
 all patches produce the same amount of food which must be shared, so for
 any given patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 individuals receive a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\sim\text{Normal}(\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{i}},\sigma_{g})\label{eq:-14}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{g}$
\end_inset

 is the mean food mass production of any patch and parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{g}$
\end_inset

 is the food mass standard deviation.
 However, as we are working with discrete mathematics, we must approximate
 this normal distribution using a pseudo-normal probability distribution
 over a grid possible food mass values 
\begin_inset Formula $g\in\boldsymbol{G}=\{0,1,...,G_{max}\}$
\end_inset

, with probabilies for each realised food mass being 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
p(g|q_{i})=\frac{1}{K}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{i}})^{2}}{2\sigma_{g}^{2}}} & \text{for} & g\in\boldsymbol{G},\end{array}\label{eq:-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K=\underset{-G}{\overset{G}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{i}})^{2}}{2\sigma_{g}^{2}}}.\label{eq:-15}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Observation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, individuals observe the resulting nutrient data and update their population
 density information states (i.e.
 their belief states).
 Assuming Bayesian optimal updating, their posterior belief is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi^{\prime}=\frac{\pi\cdot\sum_{i=I/2}^{I}p(g|q_{i})\cdot p(q_{i}|\pi)}{(1-\pi)\cdot\sum_{i=0}^{I/2}p(g|q_{i})\cdot p(q_{i}|\pi)+\pi\sum_{i=I/2}^{I}p(g|q_{i})\cdot p(q_{i}|\pi)}\label{eq:-16}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which then needs to be interpolated onto the grid of possible values of
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{1}=floor(\pi^{\prime}\cdot L)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{2}=d_{1}+1
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{2}=\pi^{\prime}\cdot L-d_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}=1-p_{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
we can then determine the state transition probability, 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\pi^{\prime}=d_{1}|\pi,g)=p_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\pi^{\prime}=d_{2}|\pi,g)=p_{2}.
\]

\end_inset

else 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each individual, choose a developmental action given their state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

, given the resident developmental strategy for that state 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Their developmental action determines the proportion of energy foraged
 that goes to the development of phenotypes of greater reproductive quality
 (e.g.
 fighter morphology), generating a transition in their size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and their reproductive quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 The resulting transition probability, 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

, between state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}\}$
\end_inset

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given the resident developmental strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

, is found be doing the following: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m,g\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m,g)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, add 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s+\Delta s,m+\Delta m|s,\pi,m,g,\kappa)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta m=max(g-\kappa,0)\label{eq:-20-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in quality state of individuals after ingesting a food mass
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, and where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta s=max(g-\Delta m-y(s,m),0),\label{eq:-19-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in size state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of
 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, given that all energy allocated to growth below the fixed and state-dependent
 maintenance cost threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

, below which all food mass is invested in maintenance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Maintenance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of the portion of energy allocated to quality, all energy below a size-dependent
 investment threshold goes to maintenance costs.
 Therefore, if investment in quality falls below this threshold there is
 a cost to survival
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(survival|g,\Delta m,\Delta s,s,m)=min(1,\frac{g-\Delta m-\Delta s}{y(s,m)})\label{eq:-44}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

such that if the individual has no energy to allocate to maintenance they
 die, where 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s)$
\end_inset

 is the size dependent maintenance cost 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y(s,m)=\frac{s^{3}\cdot m}{S^{3}\cdot M}\cdot g_{max}\label{eq:-22}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we assume mainteance costs scale by the volume of the individual and
 by the quality of soma (given their quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

), and where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{max}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum maintenance investment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Updating the States Distributions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the baseline rate of death 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 and deaths caused by lack of investment in maintenance, the posterior density
 of each state for patchs of each state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\rho_{i}^{\prime}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})=\sum_{g}\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)\cdot P(survival|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)\cdot p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)\cdot p(g|q_{i})\cdot\rho_{i}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)\label{eq:-17}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where, as a reminder, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability distribution over states after dispersal within patches
 of population size 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p(g|q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the probability of a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 in patches of size 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of transitioning to posterior information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given this food mass, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(survival|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of survival of maintenance costs given their developmental
 strategy dependent on posterior information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of transitioning to size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given the outcomes of development and given that they have survived maintenance
 costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the baseline rate of death 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 and deaths caused by lack of investment in maintenance, patches can transition
 to lower population density states.
 Therefore, given the posterior densities of individuals within patches,
 the posteior of population size of a patch of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u=q_{i}\cdot(1-\lambda)\cdot\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}\hat{\rho_{i}^{\prime}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

 The posterior distribution of patch states is therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h^{\hat{\prime}}(q_{i})=\sum_{j}^{I}h(q_{j})\cdot p_{\text{fail}}(q_{i}|q_{j})\label{eq:-23}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\text{fail}}(q_{i}|q_{j})$
\end_inset

 is the transition probability of a patch from state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 given deaths from maintenance failures, defined by interpolating 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 so that where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{1}(j)=floor(\frac{u}{n_{max}}\cdot I)\label{eq:-26}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{2}(j)=s_{1}(j)+1\label{eq:-27}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{2}(j)=(\frac{u}{n_{max}}\cdot I)-s_{1}\label{eq:-28}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}(j)=1-p_{2}(j)\label{eq:-29}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so run the following algoritm for each 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{j}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall q_{i},p_{\text{fail}}(q_{i}|q_{j})=0.\label{eq:-21}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{\text{fail}}(s_{1}(q_{j})|q_{j})=h^{\prime}(q_{j})\cdot p_{1}(j)+p_{\text{fail}}(s_{1}(q_{j})|q_{j}).\label{eq:-24}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{\text{fail}}(s_{2}(q_{j})|q_{j})=h^{\prime}(q_{j})\cdot p_{2}(j)+p_{\text{fail}}(s_{2}(q_{j})|q_{j}).\label{eq:-25}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior probability distribution over individual states for patches
 of each population density state is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho_{i}^{\prime\prime}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})=\sum_{q_{j}}h(q_{j})\cdot p_{\text{fail}}(q_{i}|q_{j})\cdot\frac{\hat{\rho_{j}^{\prime}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})}{\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}\hat{\rho_{j}^{\prime}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})}\label{eq:-30}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Patch quality perturbations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every patch undergoes momentary perturbations in quality, influenced by
 patch population density (e.g.
 the local extinction of a prey species).
 These perturbations can afford an increase or decrease in population size.
 For simplicity, we assume patches rapidly return to normal quality after
 perturbations and we assume that the probability of death is equal for
 all individuals so there is no change in the distribution of individual
 states within patches.
 We assume these perturbations have a normal distribution, although once
 again we must approximate this normal distribution to interpolate transition
 probabilities on to the grid of possible density states, where corresponding
 to the range of possible population density transitions we define the set
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{T}=\{-I,-I+1,...,I-1,I\}$
\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is the size of the population density state grid), with a pseudo-normal
 distribution over this set defining the probability of any given possible
 change in density 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\in T$
\end_inset

, defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
p(\tau)=\frac{1}{K_{\gamma}}e^{-\frac{(\tau/I)^{2}}{2\sigma_{\gamma}^{2}}} & \text{for} & \tau\in T,\end{array}\label{eq:-2-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\gamma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the standard deviation parameter for perturbations and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K_{\gamma}=\underset{t=-I}{\overset{I}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(t/I)^{2}}{2\sigma_{\gamma}^{2}}}.\label{eq:-32}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 The shift in probability generated by demographic feedback is defined as
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c=round(\frac{q_{i}}{q_{I}}\cdot\gamma)\label{eq:-33}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is the maximum risk of mass mortality events associated with population
 density.
 We can then define 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{patch}(q_{i}|q_{j})$
\end_inset

, the probability of a patch of density j to a density of i as a result
 of patch quality perturbations, as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{patch}(q_{i}|q_{j})=\begin{cases}
\text{if }i=0 & \sum_{\tau=min(I,i+c-j)}^{\tau=-I}p(\tau)\\
\text{if }0<i<I & p(\tau=min(I,i+c-j)\\
\text{if }i=I & \sum_{\tau=min(I,i+c-j)}^{\tau=I}p(\tau)
\end{cases}\label{eq:-34}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given these perturbations, the proportion of patches that are of population
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the next time step is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h^{\prime\prime}(q_{i})=\sum_{j}^{I}p_{patch}(q_{i}|q_{j})\cdot h^{\prime}(q_{j})\label{eq:-35}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Bayesian updating between growth cycles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals also know the transition probabilities between patch population
 density states at demographic stability resulting from these growth and
 death processes, and so individuals that survive to the next growth cycle
 will update their information states accordingly for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(stay)=\sum_{i=I/2}^{I}\sum_{j=I/2}^{I}\tilde{p}(q_{i}|q_{j}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(change)=\sum_{i=I/2}^{I}\sum_{j=0}^{I/2}\tilde{p}(q_{i}|q_{j}),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi^{\prime}=\pi\cdot P(stay)+(1-\pi)\cdot P(change),\label{eq:-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}(q_{i}|q_{j})$
\end_inset

 is their prior for the transition probability for a patch of population
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to a density of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 between cycles, adapted to their mean values at demographic stability over
 their evolutionary history.
 These priors are summed in the first expression, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(stay)$
\end_inset

, such that it finds the probability of a high density patch remaining high
 density and which are summed in the second expression, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(change)$
\end_inset

, such that it finds the probability of a low density patch becoming a high
 density patch.
 For the first instance, when we have not yet found demographic stability,
 we will assume that their priors are that 
\begin_inset Formula $P(stay)=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P(change)=0$
\end_inset

 (this should not have a strong influence on the long term adaptive dynamics
 assuming that the dynamics are ordered rather than chaotic).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demographic Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that with successive generations 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 that the distribution of patches over states converges on stable values,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall q_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{\ensuremath{h^{*}(q_{i})}=lim}_{\rightarrow\infty}[h(q_{i})]\label{eq:-36}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

further, we can approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 by iterating over generations until 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
tol>\sum_{i}|h(q_{i})-h^{\prime\prime}(q_{i})|\label{eq:-37}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the tolerance threshold, 
\begin_inset Formula $tol$
\end_inset

, is very small, and 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{\prime\prime}(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of population density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 after dispersal and deaths.
 Once demographic stability is reached we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}(q_{i})=h(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 and proceed with invasion analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Finding Evolutionary Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having found the demographically stable state of the metapopulation, given
 the resident strategy, we can then use the Bellman equation to find the
 best response strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $b(x(s,\pi,m))$
\end_inset

 for an invading individual.
 We then assume the best response strategy becomes the new resident strategy,
 and recalculate the demographically stable state of the metapopulation.
 The evolutionarily stable strategy (ESS) is a resident strategy for which
 invading individuals can do no better than to follow the resident strategy
 and so the ESS is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{x}(s,\pi,m)=b(x(s,\pi,m))=x(s,\pi,m).\label{eq:-38-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assuming there is a singular ESSs, we can iterating over invasions till
 the best response strategy converges on the ESS, and the starting point
 of the population will not matter.
 By sampling from multiple starting points we can test this assumption,
 and if necessary plot out the adaptive dynamics with phase portraits where
 there are multiple ESSs or oscillatory dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the best response we define a Bellman fitness equation for individuals
 in the model where 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{n=0}(s,\pi,m)=0$
\end_inset

 for all state combinations (as the individual is dead), and iterate backwards
 to the previous time step 
\begin_inset Formula $n+1$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V_{n+1}(s,\pi,m)=R(s,m)+\text{max}_{x(s,\pi,m)=\kappa}[\sum_{s_{n}}\sum_{\pi_{n}}\sum_{m_{n}}p(survival|\pi_{n})\cdot p(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n},survives|s,\pi,m,\kappa)\cdot V_{n}(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n})],\label{eq:-39-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m)$
\end_inset

 is the rate of reproduction for an individual of size 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and quality 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 (see equation 1), 
\begin_inset Formula $p(survives|s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability the individual survives between timesteps given maintenance
 costs, and the baseline probability of death, and 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n}|s,\pi,m,\kappa)$
\end_inset

 is the state transition probability to the posterior state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n}\}$
\end_inset

 of individuals of prior state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dispersal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals are born with accurate prior information regarding the propbability
 distribution over the population density states of patches 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi=\{\pi_{0}=p(q_{0})=h(q_{0}),\pi_{1}=p(q_{1})=h(q_{1}),...,\pi_{I}=p(q_{I})=h(q_{I})\}\label{eq:-3-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals then update their information state given cues for the population
 density of their patch which come from the availability of food.
 Individuals are born at size state 1 and reproductive quality state 0.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals also know the transition probabilities between patch population
 density states at demographic stability resulting from dispersal, and so
 individuals will update their information state accordingly for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi_{i}=\sum_{j}\pi_{j}\cdot\tilde{p}_{births}(q_{i}|q_{j})\label{eq:-18}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}_{births}(q_{i}|q_{j})$
\end_inset

 is the transition probability at demographic stability of a patch of population
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to a density of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, given dispersal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foraging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals observe cues for population density in the form of food availability.
 Food availability is a random variable influenced by the population density,
 all patches produce the same amount of food which must be shared, so for
 any given patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 individuals receive a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\sim\text{Normal}(\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{i}},\sigma_{g})\label{eq:-14-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{g}$
\end_inset

 is the mean food mass production of any patch and parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{g}$
\end_inset

 is the food mass standard deviation.
 However, as we are working with discrete mathematics, we must approximate
 this normal distribution using a pseudo-normal probability distribution
 over a grid possible food mass values 
\begin_inset Formula $G\in\{g_{0},g_{1},...,g_{G}\}$
\end_inset

, with probabilies for each realised food mass being 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
p(g|\pi=p(q_{i}))=\frac{1}{K}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{i}})^{2}}{2\sigma_{g}^{2}}} & \text{for} & g\in G,\end{array}\label{eq:-2-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K=\underset{-G}{\overset{G}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{i}})^{2}}{2\sigma_{g}^{2}}}.\label{eq:-15-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Observations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, individuals observe the resulting nutrient data and update their population
 density information states.
 Here we find the transition probability of individuals given these observations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)=p(p^{\prime}(q_{j})|p(q_{i}),g)=\frac{p(q_{i})\cdot p(g|p(q_{i}))}{\sum_{k}^{I}p(q_{k})\cdot p(g_{k}|p(q_{k}))}\label{eq:-16-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As observations of patch density and an individual's development are linked
 by the same random variable, food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, we define the state transition probability given individual decision making
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime},survives|s,\pi,m,\kappa)=\sum_{g}p(g|\pi)\cdot p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)\cdot p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime},survives|\kappa,s,m,\pi^{\prime},g)\label{eq:-40}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p(g|\pi)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 given the information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(g|\pi)=\sum_{i}^{I}\pi_{i}\cdot p(g|q_{i}),
\]

\end_inset

and where 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of posterior information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 after an individual of prior information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 observes a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime},survives|\kappa,s,m,\pi^{\prime},g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of posterior size 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and quality 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given the individual's developmental policy dependent on their current
 size, quality, and information states, given the food mass they received,
 and given their probability of survival given maintenance costs, all defined
 once by doing the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m,g,\kappa\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m,g,\kappa)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, define the transitions in state predicted by their developmental strategy
 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa,$
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta m=max(g-\kappa,0)\label{eq:-20-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in quality state of individuals after ingesting a food mass
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, and where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta s=max(g-\Delta m-y(s,m),0),\label{eq:-19-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in size state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of
 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, given that all energy allocated to growth below the fixed and state-dependent
 maintenance cost threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

, below which all food mass is invested in maintenance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Then add 
\begin_inset Formula $p(survival|g,\Delta m,\Delta s,s,m)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s+\Delta s,m+\Delta m,survives|s,\pi,m,g,\kappa)$
\end_inset

, where depending on the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 an individual encounters and the fraction of that mass that they invest
 in maintenance, there is a probability of death from lack of maintenance
 of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(survival|g,\Delta m,\Delta s,s,m)=min(1,\frac{g-\Delta m-\Delta s}{y(s,m)})\label{eq:-44-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

 is the size and quality dependent maintenance cost threshold to avoid death
 completely, defined as shown previously:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y(s,m)=\frac{s^{3}\cdot m}{S^{3}\cdot M}\cdot g_{max}\label{eq:-22-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So then, accounting for both the demographically stable transition probabilities
 between patch density states, and given individual decision-making, the
 probability of an individual transitioning from state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n}\}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n},survives|s,\pi,m,\kappa)=\sum_{\pi^{\prime}}p(\pi_{n}|\pi^{\prime},p_{deaths}(q_{k}|q_{i}))\cdot\sum_{\tilde{\pi}}a(s_{n},\pi^{\prime},m_{n},survives|s,\tilde{\pi},m,\kappa)\cdot p(\tilde{\pi}|\pi,p_{births}(q_{k}|q_{i}))\label{eq:-41}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where at demographic stability we found transition probabilities for patch
 density states after deaths and births, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{deaths}(q_{j}|q_{k})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{k}|q_{i})$
\end_inset

 respectively and we assume these transition probabilities are known to
 the invading individual, generating predictable transitions in their belief
 state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Depletion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals have a probability of death from patch depletion and a probability
 of death 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 from exogenous factors (e.g.
 old age).
 Given an individual's information state after foraging 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\pi}^{\prime}=\{\pi_{j=1},\pi_{j=2},...,\pi_{j=I}\}$
\end_inset

 and given the probability of patch density transition at demographic stability,
 the probability that an individual dies due to patch depletion is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(survival|\boldsymbol{\pi}^{\prime})=(1-\lambda)\cdot\sum_{j}\sum_{k}\pi_{j}\cdot(1-p_{deaths}(q_{k}|q_{j})\cdot\frac{j-k}{j})\label{eq:-45}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


